Thread exit: async-signal-safe suspend/callback signals; quit without SDL's watch lock (2026-10-14).
Applied by tools/build_sdk_from_source.py (SDK_BEHAVIOUR_PATCHES). No switch (crash fix).

Crash (Flash Kit on an M3 Pro, while the kit closed the game): EXC_BREAKPOINT "Trying to
recursively lock an os_unfair_lock" on a guest thread inside pthread_exit. TerminateTitle wakes
guest threads with SignalAllWaitableObjects and then sends each one a SIGUSR2 user-callback hint;
threads woken by the first step are already self-exiting. rex::thread::signal_handler loaded the
thread_local current_thread_ at entry (the compiler hoists the TLV thunk above the SIGUSR2
early-out), and on Darwin every thread_local is a dyld TLV block allocated by malloc on first
access and freed by a pthread key destructor. A signal landing during pthread_exit's TSD cleanup
(which holds the malloc metapool lock while freeing) re-instantiated the block: malloc ->
recursive unfair lock -> abort. __thread / initial-exec does not help on Mach-O (same TLV path).

- threading_posix.cpp: the handler finds its thread through a pthread key (pthread_getspecific
  reads the TSD slot, no allocation); every exit path (start routine return, Terminate on self,
  main-thread Thread::Exit) blocks SIGUSR1/SIGUSR2 and clears the slot first, so later signals
  stay pending and die with the thread. QueueUserCallback and Suspend skip a kFinished thread and
  send the signal with state_mutex_ held (an exiting thread blocks the signals before it takes
  that mutex to publish kFinished, so the target still exists): no more "QueueUserCallback:
  signal delivery failed (3)" (ESRCH on a dead thread; ~6 per close before).
- kernel_state.cpp: TerminateTitle does not signal targets that already cleared running_, and
  names the threads still running when its 200 ms cooperative-exit timeout expires.
- windowed_app_context_sdl.cpp: WatchEvent handles SDL_EVENT_QUIT inside SDL's event-watch
  dispatch, so SDL's watcher lock is held for all of OnClosing/TerminateTitle; a guest thread
  queueing UI work (CallInUIThread -> wakeup SDL_PushEvent, e.g. XamInputGetState's keyboard
  refresh) blocked on it and became a straggler. While a quit is in progress, wakeups from
  non-UI threads are skipped (the function stays queued; one wakeup is sent if the close is
  vetoed).
- thread.h: REX_THREAD_EXIT_SIGNAL_SAFE (tests/thread_exit_signal_tests.cpp skips without it).

